The environment (in MuJoCo) simulates a Unitree Go2 quadruped robot performing a crawling gait. The robot must stay low to the ground and move forward in the +x direction.

The policy acts as a high-level trajectory generator. The DMP weights define a cyclic 2D trajectory in PCA latent space, which is then mapped to 12-joint angle trajectories via PCA inverse transform. This joint trajectory is replayed cyclically on the robot for 1000 simulation steps using PD position control.

The PCA latent space was fitted on a dataset of manually designed crawling poses. The initial DMP weights produce a circular trajectory in this space, which corresponds to a basic crawling gait. The optimizer's job is to find better weights that produce a more effective crawl.

The reward measures forward progress in the +x direction, penalized by lateral drift (y movement) and excessive joint velocities. The episode terminates early if the robot's base height goes outside [0.14, 0.20] meters, or if roll/pitch angles exceed 50 degrees, indicating a fall or flip.

Higher reward means the robot crawled farther forward while staying stable and low.
